 — every value listed, no means substituted**.<br>**CPU-only (`-dev none`, the true kernel-vs-kernel comparison).** *mixed* prompt tok/s — ON `612.81/619.65/629.06/638.73/643.72/647.85/648.09/655.15/670.67` vs OFF `142.15/143.14/143.35/143.81/150.85/152.44/152.65/152.90/153.16` (**+326.7%** median, ranges DISJOINT); *mixed* decode tok/s — ON `49.23/49.49/49.64/49.67/49.71/49.72/49.75/49.77/49.80` vs OFF `39.46/40.16/40.21/40.31/41.46/41.60/41.67/41.69/41.76` (**+19.9%**, DISJOINT). *all-Q2_0* prompt tok/s — ON `602.65/639.27/640.38/657.49/738.80/740.08/761.58/769.50/772.82` vs OFF `128.48/129.33/129.72/135.24/136.35/136.73/136.98/136.98/137.44` (**+441.8%**, DISJOINT); *all-Q2_0* decode tok/s — ON `51.24/51.27/51.31/51.39/51.39/51.54/51.56/51.58/51.60` vs OFF `38.71/39.28/39.71/40.14/41.59/41.62/41.65/41.75/41.79` (**+23.6%**, DISJOINT).<br>**Noise check passes explicitly**: largest within-arm spread is `3.08` tok/s (decode) and `170.17` tok/s (all-Q2_0 prompt ON) against between-arm gaps of `9.80` and `602.45`; ON and OFF ranges do not overlap in any of the eight comparisons. This is the opposite of the earlier `+31%` slot that was noise.<br>**Vulkan-enabled (default build, `-dev` unset) — the merge-relevant caveat.** Decode still wins: *mixed* ON `49.32/49.33/49.34/49.37/49.49/49.99/50.00/50.21/50.21` vs OFF `39.70/39.98/40.23/40.84/41.20/41.28/41.62/41.68/41.74` (**+20.1%**); *all-Q2_0* ON `51.11/51.20/51.38/51.46/51.66/51.76/51.76/51.78/51.96` vs OFF `39.99/40.06/40.10/40.13/40.25/40.31/40.31/41.45/41.61` (**+28.4%**). But **prompt processing collapses**: *mixed* ON `213.82/216.32/216.77/219.28/220.69/221.12/221.83/241.40/258.81` vs OFF `952.81/956.58/959.42/959.92/961.95/966.47/967.16/969.51/971.34` (**-77.1%**); *all-Q2_0* ON `90.33/94.46/99.75/114.08/114.27/116.23/116.24/117.51/118.59` vs OFF `927.01/944.32/950.46/951.14/957.05/958.06/961.67/965.85/972.70` (**-88.1%**). **Cause, evidenced not assumed:** weights in the `CPU_REPACK` buffer are no longer eligible for large-batch GPU offload. All-Q2_0 ON logs `Vulkan0 compute buffer size = 5.26 MiB / Vulkan_Host = 34.01 MiB, graph splits = 286`, while OFF logs `Vulkan0 = 37.72 MiB / Vulkan_Host = 5.51 MiB, graph splits = 335` — the batch matmuls that ran on the iGPU move back to the CPU. Decode (`bs=1`, `graph splits = 1`) is CPU-side in both arms, which is why the decode win survives. **Therefore: default OFF on a Vulkan build; ON is the right default only for a CPU-only path.**<br><br>**Not attempted, recorded:** wiring these kernels to `GGML_TYPE_Q2_0_G128`. The base block layouts are byte-identical, but this fork has **no** `block_q2_0_g128x4` type and no g128 repack path, and the existing g64 `block_q2_0x4` is 72 B — dispatching type-43 into it would be silent wrong math. A real g128 path needs its own x4 struct (`d[4] + qs[128]` = 136 B), g128 repack/make functions, block-size-parameterised kernels, separate dispatch and scalar-equivalence testing. Future slot.<br><br>**HTTP 500 reproduction, corrected.** The server guard "The model produced output that does not match the expected Content-only format" was previously recorded as an ON-arm, all-Q2_0 phenomenon. It is **not repack-related**: on a long repetitive prompt it fires in **all 12 performance arms — ON and OFF, both models** (`CPU_REPACK = 84.16/95.98 MiB` and `0.0 MiB` alike), while the short prompt passes the guard in every arm. It is a response-format guard on degenerate output; generation completes and the server stays alive, so timings remain readable from `slot print_timing`.<br><br>**Still unexercised:** GEMV bit-exactness against `vec_dot` in isolation (`-ub 1` still the only clean lever and it remains untested here); the generic `repack.cpp` GEMM fix, which is unreachable on this box because the AVX512 branch returns first; and every Arm/NEON arm. Evidence: `research/local-inference/trial-evidence/110/vnni-g64-fix/` (NaN fix) and `research/local-inference/trial-evidence/110/vnni-adjudication/` (adjudication + performance). |
 | `GGML_ARIFI_ROCMFPX_FORMATS` (CMake) | **OFF.** Build-time option in `ggml/CMakeLists.txt`, propagated as a PUBLIC compile definition on `ggml-base`. It is deliberately **not** an environment variable: the CPU dot products for these types are entries in `type_traits_cpu[]` (`ggml/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c`), selected at link time, so a runtime toggle could not cover them - the exact defect that made the VNNI slot's first three A/Bs worthless. Two things stay unconditional regardless of the flag: the type-ids in `ggml/include/ggml.h` (the enum is ABI and must not move with a build flag) and `GGML_TYPE_COUNT = 256`. | Compiles the six ROCmFPX serialized weight formats and their whole CPU path: block structs and reference quant/dequant (`ggml/rocmfp4/`, `ggml/rocmfpx/`), `type_traits[]` rows, `type_traits_cpu[]` rows and four scalar `vec_dot`s, the `--check-tensors` row validators in `ggml-quants.c`, six of the seven type-id dispatch sites in `ggml-cpu/ops.cpp`, the `ggml_quantize_chunk` arms, and the llama-side quantizer wiring. Ids adopted verbatim: `100 Q4_0_ROCMFP4` (18 B/32), `101 Q4_0_ROCMFP4_FAST` (17 B/32), `102 Q6_0_ROCMFPX` (26 B/32), `103 Q8_0_ROCMFPX` (33 B/32), `104 Q3_0_ROCMFPX` (14 B/32), `107 Q2_0_ROCMFPX` (10 B/32). It does **not** compile any GPU kernel for these types - Vulkan and CUDA support are separate slots - and it does not port ROCmFPX's quantizer **recipes** (`_LEAN`, `_COHERENT`, `_STRIX`, `_AGENT`), which are a distinct mechanism. | Turn it ON to read or write ROCmFPX GGUFs. On a Vulkan build these types have no GPU pipeline, so `ggml_backend_vk_device_supports_op()` returns false for them and every matmul falls back to CPU - that is why the default is OFF on the 780M, and why Strix Halo / discrete AMD are ON-path only once the HIP and Vulkan slots land. OFF is safe, not lossy: the file is **refused, never guessed at**. | **Correctness proven in both arms on a real artifact. Performance UNMEASURED - no ON/OFF benchmark was run and none should be quoted.**<br><br>**Correction to the slot brief:** it stated there is no ROCmFP-format model on disk and that ON-path execution was therefore likely unverifiable. That is **false**. `<MODELS>/rocmfpx-trial/` holds two native ROCmFP4 GGUFs; `ornith-9b-mtp-kl-Q4_0_ROCMFP4_COHERENT.gguf` was probed directly from its header and carries **257 tensors at type-id 100**, 1 Q6_K and 184 F32, with `general.file_type = 102` and arch `qwen35`. The ON path was verified against it, not by proxy.<br><br>**ON.** llama-server per F-085, `-ngl 0 -c 512`, gate-ON build: the model loaded in **7.0 s**, `/props` reported `model_ftype = Q4_0_ROCMFP4_COHERENT`, and `/completion` on "The capital of France is" returned " Paris." and then repeated the sentence, at **3.24 tok/s** predicted. A chat request produced coherent multi-step reasoning. Execution ran through `rocmfp4_vec_dot_q4_0_q8_0` on CPU, as designed - Vulkan declined the type.<br><br>**OFF.** The identical file on the identical build with the option OFF is refused, verbatim: `gguf_init_from_reader: tensor 'output.weight' of type 100 ((null)) has 4096 elements per row, not a multiple of block size (0)`, then `failed to load model`, exit 1. That is the universal `blck_size == 0` backstop at `ggml/src/gguf.cpp:715`, not new code - which is what makes raising `GGML_TYPE_COUNT` to 256 safe despite the sparse 44..255 range it opens.<br><br>**Reference conformance.** Both ROCmFPX self-test suites were compiled against the ported sources with our own MinGW gcc and pass: `test_rocmfpx.c` (ROCmFP3/6/8 block and row sizes, bpw, mse, imatrix-weighted mse) and `test_rocmfp2_reference.c` (**32520 checks** - layout, packed-byte decode, UE4M3 boundaries, metadata validation, deterministic ties, Python golden vectors, non-finite rejection; fingerprint `78389c1c8a0a57a2`).<br><br>**No regression, OFF arm, same binary.** g128 still works: the native type-43 `Ternary-Bonsai-27B-Abliterated-LowDeg-Q2_0.g128.gguf` loads and answers "The capital of France is" with " Paris." (0.65 tok/s, `-ngl 0`, a 27B fully on CPU under memory pressure). VNNI still works: on `qwen2.5-0.5b-light-q8_0-4xffndownQ2_0-g64.gguf` with `-ngl 0 --no-host -c 1024 -t 8`, `GGML_ARIFI_VNNI_REPACK=1` produces `CPU_REPACK model buffer size = 4.68 MiB` with **286** ineligible tensors, and unset produces **no** `CPU_REPACK` line with **290** - reproducing the VNNI row's own documented numbers exactly, with token-identical coherent output in both arms and `CPU_Mapped = 625.75 MiB` in both.<br><br>**Cost of the un-gated part.** `GGML_TYPE_COUNT` 44 -> 256 is paid in both arms. It grows every `[GGML_TYPE_COUNT]`-dimensioned array, of which the Vulkan backend has by far the most (twelve `bool[COUNT]`, five `vk_matmul_pipeline2[COUNT]`, three `vk_pipeline[2][COUNT][8]`). The allocation contract's estimate is ~1.5 KB of `vk_device_struct` per type-id, i.e. **~320 KB more per Vulkan device** - an **estimate carried forward, not a measurement**; `sizeof(vk_matmul_pipeline2)` was not compiled here either. Device init also runs two `for (i = 0; i < GGML_TYPE_COUNT; ++i)` loops (`ggml-vulkan.cpp:4025`, `:6625`) over 256 ids instead of 44; both bodies are trivial, and `ggml_vk_matmul_shmem_support()` was read to confirm it has a `default: break` and performs no type-traits lookup, so an unimplemented id cannot fault it. **Not measured:** device-init wall time before versus after.<br><br>**Known consequence, recorded not fixed.** `tests/test-gguf.cpp` draws random types as `rng() % GGML_TYPE_COUNT` and skips those whose `type_size` is 0. Raising the ceiling to 256 cuts its effective random-type coverage by roughly 5.8x. The test still passes by construction; the coverage loss is real and belongs to whoever next touches that file.<br><br>**Not attempted, named:** GPU kernels for these types (ROCmFPX ships Vulkan dequant shaders and CUDA `mmq`/`mmvq`/`vecdotq`/`fattn` instances; both are separate slots); the ROCmFPX quantizer recipes; a round-trip quantization through our own `llama-quantize` into any of these types; whether `rocmfpx.c`'s 2-bit path is byte-identical to the frozen `rocmfp2_reference.c` (ROCmFPX's own `test_rocmfpx.c` covers only fp3/fp6/fp8); and any perplexity or throughput comparison against `Q4_0` or `Q4_K_M`. Evidence: this row plus the commit trailers on `topic/rocmfp-formats`. |
 | `GGML_ARIFI_G128_MMV_ROWS` | **unset = the shipped value (2 rows).** Any positive integer overrides rows-per-workgroup for the `Q2_0_G128` Vulkan mul_mat_vec pipeline only. Added 2026-07-30 (lane-110G). | Scope: the **integer-dot** (`q8_1`) `mul_mat_vec` registration for `GGML_TYPE_Q2_0_G128` and nothing else. That is the registration this format actually uses — `ggml_vk_should_use_mmvq()` returns true for it on AMD at `k >= 2048` — and it is a different knob from the two *dequant* registrations, which take rows from `rm_stdq`. Scoped to g128 rather than raising `rm_kq_int` itself, which is shared with `Q2_0`, `Q2_K` and several K-quants this box has no evidence for. | **Do not turn it on.** It exists so the question could be asked on one binary with an env flip instead of two builds, and the answer was no. Keep it for re-asking on other hardware, where `rm_kq_int` is equally untuned. | **MEASURED 2026-07-30, and it is a NULL — the shipped value wins.** Which pipeline is live was established first, by measurement rather than by reading: `GGML_VK_DISABLE_MMVQ` moves decode **34.11 → 28.39 tok/s (+20.1%, ranges DISJOINT**, three interleaved replicates, one binary), so the integer-dot path is the one serving this format. Its rows come from `2 * rm_kq_int`, and **`rm_kq_int` is 1 on every device — no vendor branch raises it** (AMD_GCN raises `rm_stdq`/`rm_kq`/`rm_stdq_int`, Intel raises `rm_stdq`/`rm_stdq_int`), so this pipeline has run 2 rows per workgroup everywhere it has ever run.<br>**Sweep** (RIG-A, `Ternary-Bonsai-8B-Q2_0.g128`, `-ngl 99 -c 2048`, 519-token prefill, `n_predict 128`, three interleaved replicates, control reproduced within 0%): decode **2 rows `34.17`** · 4 rows `33.59` (**−1.7%, OVERLAPPING**) · 8 rows `33.38` (**−2.3%, OVERLAPPING**). Neither is a result; the untuned default is already right here.<br>**Correctness held**: generated output is byte-identical across all three row counts, which matters because a wrong row count in this kernel is silent wrong math rather than a crash.<br>**One unexplained measurement, kept rather than smoothed:** *prompt* at 4 rows collapses to `174.63` from `310.85` (tight range, not noise) while 8 rows returns to `291.12` — non-monotonic and not accounted for. The prediction that prompt would be flat was wrong: `should_use_mmvq` also returns true for `n > 1`, so this pipeline serves small batches and does touch prefill. Evidence: `research/local-inference/trial-evidence/110/g128-kernels/rows/` and `.../mmvq/`. |
 | `GGML_ARIFI_Q6K_XFOLD` | **ON when unset** (`=0` opts out; any other value keeps it on). Specialization constant 4 of `mul_mat_vec_q6_k.comp`, passed at both Q6_K dmmv registrations (`ggml-vulkan.cpp:7100` f32_f32, `:7166` f16_f32). Added 2026-09-14 (lane-236 / R48c). One binary serves both arms. | Scope: the **f32 dequant** `mul_mat_vec` path for `GGML_TYPE_Q6_K` and nothing else. Arm 1 does two things: (a) loads the superblock's four `data_b` `vec4`s once per superblock instead of once per row, reusing them across all `NUM_ROWS` rows; (b) removes q6_K's per-weight `- 32` bias from the decode and applies it as one `fma` per sub-block per column, `sum(b*u) - 32*sum(b)`. (b) carries its own n-GATE at `NUM_COLS <= 3` — counted, it removes 16 subtractions per row but adds 4 fma per row **per column**, so it inverts at width 5+. (a) has no crossover. Orthogonal to `GGML_VK_Q6K_DIRECT_SCALES`: the fold is applied identically in both of that constant's arms. | **Do not change it without a pairing.** It was registered ON on counted load and ALU deltas (n=1: −13.3% loads, −8.7% ALU); it **stays** ON on the measured pairing in the next cell — no q6_K row below −3% at any width, plus the n=6 dip removal — not on a width-1 win, which the pairing did not deliver on both FFN shapes. Set `=0` to back it out in the field without a rebuild — the intended recovery if a width regresses, since arm 0 is the shipped order byte for byte. | **MEASURED 2026-09-14 (lane-236 / R48c), and it is kept on no-harm plus one dip removal, NOT on a width-1 win.** Pairing: `research/local-inference/lane-evidence/2026-09-14-lane-234-r48-kernels/32-paired-r48cq6k.txt`, 3 quiet interleaved rounds, one binary env-flipped (arm A = `GGML_ARIFI_Q6K_XFOLD=0`, arm B = default ON), `test-backend-ops perf -o MUL_MAT -b Vulkan0` at the three 27B decode shapes plus legacy4096, n = 1..8. Result on the q6_K rows: **n = 1..5, 7, 8 is a wash, span −3.0% to +5.6%** (best +5.61% ffn-down n=1; worst −2.97% legacy4096 n=4, −2.91% legacy4096 n=8); **n = 6 is +26% to +38%** (+38.03% embd-out, +27.53% ffn-down, +26.28% ffn-gate-up, 3/3 rounds each) — arm A has a dip at `NUM_COLS == 6` (embd-out 31.10 GB/s at n=6 against 67.16 at n=5 and 53.50 at n=7) that arm B removes. **No q6_K row is below −3%.** Controls: `q4_K` and `q5_K` shaders are untouched and their three rows below −3% have overlapping per-round triples (noise, reported unfiltered). **Acceptance rule 1 of the report (§6: ≥ +5% median at n=1 on BOTH FFN shapes) is NOT met** — ffn-down +5.61% passes, ffn-gate-up **+3.69%** does not. The row is kept on rule 2 (veto: no q6_K row worse than −3%; worst −2.97%) plus the n=6 dip removal. Correctness: `test-backend-ops -o MUL_MAT` clean in all four constant arms (1688/1688 at XFOLD=1; the single 248320x5120 n=1 failure seen at XFOLD=0 is a pre-existing intermittent defect that also fires 3/60 on the pure-upstream shader path, `r48c-evidence/42-*`, not this arm's). **Not bit-identical to arm 0** — the `-32` moves out of the fma chain into a single `fma(-32.0, xs, sum)`, which reorders float sums; `FLOAT_TYPE` is `float` for every mat-vec pipeline (`vulkan-shaders-gen.cpp:846`), never `float16_t`, so the cancellation is in fp32 and expected well inside the MUL_MAT NMSE gate. **Named risk:** arm 1 holds `NUM_COLS x 4` `vec4`s live across the row loop (128 VGPRs of activation at `NUM_COLS = 8`) — the exact shape that spilled in lane-230, `mul_mat_vec_sx8.comp:60-71`, losing −45% at n=5 and −77% at n=8. That risk did not materialise: the widest rows (n=7, n=8) are inside the wash band. Pairing plan and acceptance bars: `OPUS-R48C-Q6K-MATVEC-REPORT.md` §6, which also records that rule 1 was not met. |
